{
 "method_card": {
  "steps": [
   "Wave equation: identify two of {v, f, λ} and solve for the third using v = fλ",
   "Density: identify two of {ρ, m, V} and solve for the third using ρ = m ÷ V",
   "Half-life: find n = total time ÷ half-life period, then halve the initial activity n times",
   "For density problems, check unit consistency — convert cm³ to m³ if needed (÷ 1 000 000)",
   "State the answer with the correct unit every time"
  ],
  "title": "Waves, Half-Life and Density",
  "content": "<p>Three distinct recall equations from different areas of physics — all appear every year and are straightforward once the equations are secure.</p><p><strong>Recall equations:</strong></p><ul><li>\\(v = f\\lambda\\) — wave speed (m/s) = frequency (Hz) × wavelength (m)</li><li>\\(\\rho = \\dfrac{m}{V}\\) — density (kg/m³) = mass (kg) ÷ volume (m³)</li></ul><p><strong>Half-life (exponential decay — no single formula needed for GCSE):</strong> After each half-life, the count rate (or number of undecayed nuclei) halves. Strategy: count how many half-lives fit in the given time, then halve repeatedly. For multi-step questions, construct a table:</p><table style=\"border-collapse:collapse;width:100%;font-size:0.9em\"><tr style=\"background:#f1f5f9\"><th style=\"padding:4px 8px;border:1px solid #cbd5e1\">Number of half-lives</th><th style=\"padding:4px 8px;border:1px solid #cbd5e1\">Activity remaining</th></tr><tr><td style=\"padding:4px 8px;border:1px solid #cbd5e1\">0</td><td style=\"padding:4px 8px;border:1px solid #cbd5e1\">Initial</td></tr><tr><td style=\"padding:4px 8px;border:1px solid #cbd5e1\">1</td><td style=\"padding:4px 8px;border:1px solid #cbd5e1\">½ of initial</td></tr><tr><td style=\"padding:4px 8px;border:1px solid #cbd5e1\">2</td><td style=\"padding:4px 8px;border:1px solid #cbd5e1\">¼ of initial</td></tr><tr><td style=\"padding:4px 8px;border:1px solid #cbd5e1\">3</td><td style=\"padding:4px 8px;border:1px solid #cbd5e1\">⅛ of initial</td></tr></table><p><strong>Unit checks:</strong><br> Wave equation: wavelength in metres, frequency in Hz (= per second), speed in m/s. Density: mass in kg, volume in m³ (note: 1 cm³ = 1 × 10⁻⁶ m³; 1 g/cm³ = 1000 kg/m³).</p>"
 },
